---
<DocsLayout title="Kibana Dashboards" description="Visual dashboards for monitoring honeypot activity" section="Observability" sidebar={sidebar} toc={toc}>
  <p>
    Ten saved dashboards cover alert triage, aggregate threat posture, decisioning, session behaviour,
    attack patterns, threat intelligence, infrastructure logs, pipeline and host health, pool capacity and
    detection coverage, plus an embed-only timeline dashboard used for evidence capture. All of them are generated from Python and committed as NDJSON, so the same definition can be
    rebuilt offline or imported into a live Kibana. The <code>kibana_dashboards_setup</code> service re-imports
    the NDJSON on every <code>docker compose up</code> with <code>overwrite=true</code>, so the set is
    idempotent and self-healing, and it sets the Web Traffic dashboard as Kibana's landing page.
  </p>
  <CardGrid columns={3}>
    <DocCard title="10 registered views" icon="chart" tag="Operational" description="Alerts, web threat overview, decisions and decay, session analysis, attack patterns, threat intelligence, infrastructure logs, pipeline health, pool capacity and detection coverage." meta="dashboard-* IDs" />
    <DocCard title="Timeline capture" icon="eye" tag="Evidence" description="A dedicated embed-mode dashboard renders a pattern's request timeline before a screenshot is taken." meta="per-pattern" />
    <DocCard title="Review friendly" icon="branch" tag="Code-managed" description="Layouts live in Python, so changes are reviewable and exportable rather than click-only state." meta="NDJSON in git" />
  </CardGrid>

  <h2 id="registry">Dashboard Registry</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Dashboard ID</th><th>Title</th><th>Answers the question…</th></tr>
    <tr><td>dashboard-ids-alerts</td><td>Honeypot: IDS Alerts (Suricata)</td><td>What did the network IDS see? Severity over time, categories, protocols, targeted ports, top signatures and source IPs.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>dashboard-web-threat-overview</td><td>Honeypot: Web Traffic & Threat Overview</td><td>What was requested and how was it answered? The Top URIs table marks every URI <code>attack_pattern</code> or <code>safe</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>dashboard-threat-decisions</td><td>Honeypot: Threat Decisions & Decay</td><td>Why did each request go where it went? Request score vs the decayed score carried from the session, against the threshold; decay state, offenses and routing reasons.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>dashboard-session-analysis</td><td>Honeypot: Session Analysis</td><td>How did each session behave? Per-session volume, duration, peak score, offenses and sophistication class.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>dashboard-attack-patterns</td><td>Honeypot: Attack Patterns</td><td>What did requests match? Attack patterns and CVEs, the URIs behind them, security events, honeytokens and top attacking IPs.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>dashboard-threat-intel</td><td>Honeypot: Threat Intelligence</td><td>What does external reputation say? Threat-intelligence integrations and flagged addresses.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>(infrastructure)</td><td>Honeypot: Infrastructure &amp; Ops Logs</td><td>How healthy is the stack itself? Docker container volume by service and stream, nginx's own <code>info</code>/<code>warn</code>/<code>error</code>/<code>crit</code> levels over time, Redis activity and a raw infrastructure log feed.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>(pipeline)</td><td>Honeypot: Pipeline &amp; Host Health</td><td>Is the observability pipeline keeping up? See below.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>dashboard-pool-capacity</td><td>Honeypot: Pool &amp; Capacity</td><td>How does the honeypot pool fleet scale, and how fast? See below.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>dashboard-pattern-coverage</td><td>Honeypot: Detection Coverage</td><td>Which attack patterns is the honeypot currently set up to catch? See below.</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>The IDS Alerts dashboard also shows the parsed Suricata fields: the rule SIDs (<code>signature_id</code> and action) that fired, the URLs targeted in HTTP alerts, and how many distinct network flows (<code>community_id</code>) the alerts span.</p>
  <p>Each dashboard is built via <code>_dashboard(dash_id, title, description, layout)</code> and registered as <code>("dashboard", did, body)</code> objects in <code>saved_objects/honeypot-dashboards.ndjson</code>, all built on the <code>honeypot-*</code> data view whose time field is <code>timestamp</code>.</p>
  <div class="admonition note">
    <div class="admonition-title">What powers the decision panels</div>
    <p>The Threat Decisions &amp; Decay panels read fields the reverse proxy writes to its <code>security</code>
    access log (<code>log_format security</code> in <code>nginx.conf</code>, filled by
    <code>router_rules.decision_trace()</code>): <code>request_score</code>, <code>decayed_score</code>,
    <code>carried_score</code>, <code>offenses</code>, <code>decay_state</code>, <code>uri_class</code>,
    <code>patterns</code> and <code>route_reason</code>. Vector parses them into typed Elasticsearch fields — see
    <a href="/docs/observability/siem">SIEM &amp; Log Pipeline</a>.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="new-dashboards">Pipeline, Pool and Coverage Dashboards</h2>
  <p>These three bring data from the operator dashboard app into Kibana, so it can be queried without the app.</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Dashboard</th><th>Source data</th><th>Panels</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Pipeline &amp; Host Health</td><td><code>honeypot-vector_metrics-*</code> and <code>honeypot-host_metrics-*</code></td><td>Both Vector shippers' <code>internal_metrics</code> (events in/out, Elasticsearch-sink buffer fill, errored and discarded events, told apart by <code>vector_role</code>) and the edge host's CPU load, memory, network and disk. Series are split by metric <code>name</code>. Empty until the metrics pipeline is deployed. The same data the app's Log Pipeline page watches.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Pool &amp; Capacity</td><td><code>honeypot-pool_state-*</code> and <code>honeypot-pool_events-*</code></td><td>Ready, free, waiting and building pools over time, the mix of pool states, per-pool build latency, per-session hand-off wait, and the edge host's load and memory as the pool manager sees them. Empty until the edge stack is redeployed.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Detection Coverage</td><td><code>honeypot-patterns</code> (<code>log_type: patterns</code>)</td><td>Enabled versus disabled patterns, by category, severity and kind; which were toggled away from the catalogue default; the full enabled-pattern list with its Suricata SID coverage. This is configuration, not traffic.</td></tr>
  </table>
  <h3>How the data gets there</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pool state.</strong> <code>pool_manager</code> writes its live state and a latency event log as JSON lines to the <code>pool_state_logs</code> volume (<code>POOL_STATE_EXPORT_DIR</code>, snapshot every <code>POOL_STATE_SNAPSHOT_SECONDS</code>, default 30). <code>vector_outbound</code> mounts the volume read-only and tails it, so the data ships to the SIEM like any other log. The writer is <code>images/pool_manager/state_export.py</code>; the image <code>COPY</code>s its modules by name, so a new module has to be added to the Dockerfile.</li>
    <li><strong>Host metrics.</strong> Vector's <code>host_metrics</code> source reads the host's <code>/proc</code> and <code>/sys</code>, bind-mounted read-only into <code>vector_outbound</code> and selected with the <code>PROCFS_ROOT</code> / <code>SYSFS_ROOT</code> environment variables.</li>
    <li><strong>Detection patterns.</strong> The pattern catalogue and its enable/disable overlay live only in the operator dashboard app, so the app pushes a snapshot into Elasticsearch (one document per pattern, in <code>honeypot-patterns</code>). SIEM Analytics does this once automatically after the first successful Kibana load, and again whenever you press <strong>Send Patterns to SIEM</strong>. The dashboard stays empty until the first push.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="clear">Clearing Elasticsearch</h2>
  <p>The SIEM Analytics page has a <strong>Clear Elasticsearch</strong> button. It permanently deletes every
  <code>honeypot-*</code> document in Elasticsearch, after a confirmation dialog, and reports how many documents
  were removed. Use it to start a test run from empty dashboards. It cannot be undone, and it does not touch the
  dashboards or data view themselves.</p>

  <h2 id="building">Building and Exporting</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Offline build.</strong> <code>python3 siem/kibana/build_dashboards.py --ndjson</code> regenerates
    <code>saved_objects/honeypot-dashboards.ndjson</code> from the Python definitions. It is idempotent and needs no
    running Kibana.</li>
    <li><strong>Live import.</strong> The same script can push the objects into a running Kibana through the Saved
    Objects API, using <code>KIBANA_URL</code>, <code>ELASTIC_USERNAME</code> and <code>ELASTIC_PASSWORD</code>. On
    a normal start, <code>kibana_dashboards_setup</code> does this for you.</li>
    <li><strong>Export after live edits.</strong> If you change panels by hand in Kibana,
    <code>python3 siem/kibana/export_dashboards.py</code> writes the live objects back to NDJSON so the next build
    or import does not overwrite them.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Inside the operator dashboard, the <a href="/docs/getting-started/dashboard#monitor">SIEM Analytics</a> page
  opens these dashboards with autologin and can export them to PDF.</p>
  <p>Every new dashboard must be added in three places or the tests fail: <code>DASHBOARD_IDS</code> in <code>export_dashboards.py</code>, the bookmark list in <code>dashboard/ui/page_kibana.py</code>, and the per-page notes in <code>dashboard/core/kibana_report.py</code>.</p>

  <h2 id="timeline">Pattern Report Timeline Dashboard</h2>
  <p>A dedicated pattern report timeline dashboard (<code>pattern-report-request-timeline-dashboard</code>) is used in embed mode to capture screenshots of request timelines for specific patterns.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Automates a login process (via autologin script if configured) to access Kibana.</li>
    <li>Imports the necessary saved objects using the Kibana API.</li>
    <li>Loads the specific timeline dashboard in <strong>embed mode</strong> for a given time window.</li>
    <li>Waits for a rendering signal (checking for a "render complete" flag) before capturing a screenshot.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="modules">Dashboard Python Module Reference</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Module</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Key Functions</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>siem/kibana/build_dashboards.py</code></td><td>Generates dashboard objects and visualizations into NDJSON</td><td>_dashboard(), dashboard registration</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>siem/kibana/export_dashboards.py</code></td><td>Exports live dashboard objects from Kibana to local NDJSON</td><td>Export by dashboard ID list</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>dashboard/core/kibana_timeline.py</code></td><td>Defines structure and payload for request timeline visualization</td><td>DASHBOARD_ID, _dashboard_body(), NDJSON generation</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>dashboard/ui/kibana_timeline_capture.py</code></td><td>Client-side workflow for live dashboard capture</td><td>Autologin, Kibana API import, embed-mode loading, screenshot capture</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2 id="honest">Honest Assessment</h2>
  <p><strong>Strengths:</strong> Offline generation is idempotent. Export workflow preserves manual panel edits. Timeline capture enables per-pattern visual evidence.</p>
  <p><strong>Limitations:</strong> Dashboard generation and export are separate scripts with potential drift. Timeline capture depends on Kibana auth setup. Layout defined in Python code (not editable JSON). No automated dashboard health monitoring. The <code>honeypot-docker-*</code> index currently ingests nothing — Docker container labels trigger an Elasticsearch field-mapping conflict (<code>label.com.docker.compose.project</code>), a pre-existing issue out of scope for the nginx/Suricata dashboards.</p>
</DocsLayout>
